{
  // Dependency-vulnerability gate for CI (replaces the bare `npm audit --audit-level=high`,
  // which has no way to record a reasoned exception).
  //
  // The gate stays sharp: anything high/critical that is NOT listed below fails the build.
  // `allowlist` is per-advisory (GHSA id), never per-severity — we do not lower the level.
  //
  // Adding an entry requires, in this file: (1) the advisory id, (2) why it does not apply to
  // THIS codebase, measured — not assumed, (3) an expiry date, (4) what removes the need for it.
  // An expired entry is a task, not a renewal: re-check it, do not extend it by reflex.
  "$schema": "https://github.com/IBM/audit-ci/raw/main/docs/schema.json",
  "moderate": false,
  "high": true,
  "critical": true,
  // ── Removed 2026-08-08: GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg (brace-expansion) ──────────────────────────
  // The entry was granted on a premise that was **false**. It read "v1 stops at 1.1.16 and v2
  // at 2.1.3, so those lines have no fixed release". The advisory's own data says otherwise:
  //
  //   gh api /advisories/GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg --jq '.vulnerabilities[]|"\(.package.name) \(.vulnerable_version_range) → \(.first_patched_version)"'
  //   brace-expansion <1.1.17 → 1.1.17 | >=2.0.0 <2.1.3 → 2.1.3 | >=4.0.0 <5.0.8 → 5.0.8
  //
  // Every line had a patch. `2.1.3` — the version this repo had pinned — was already the fixed
  // one; the allowlist had been excusing an advisory the tree no longer had. Rule 4 of
  // `docs/development/dependency-audit.md` ("prefer the fix") applied all along, and #573 took
  // it: `brace-expansion@1: ^1.1.18` / `@2: ^2.1.4` / `@5: ^5.0.9`, which also closes the
  // follow-up GHSA-rgw5-rvv9-x895. audit-ci itself printed "Consider not allowlisting advisory:
  // GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg" once the versions moved.
  //
  // 🔴 The lesson that outlives the entry — **read the advisory, not the note about it.** A
  // reason written once is a snapshot; upstream backports patches into old lines afterwards.
  // Re-run the `gh api` line above at expiry instead of re-reading the prose.
  //
  // 🔴 The override shape survives the removal. Do NOT collapse `brace-expansion@1/@2/@5` into
  // a flat `brace-expansion: ^5.x`. That forces v5 onto `minimatch@3`, which expects the v1 CJS
  // export and dies with `TypeError: expand is not a function`. It happened here (#530 → #538)
  // and **lint stayed green**, because minimatch@3 only reaches brace-expansion for patterns
  // containing `{...}` — a path our config never hits. `npm run check:overrides` probes exactly
  // that, on every copy on disk.
  //
  // Still true and still worth knowing (re-measured 2026-08-08): brace-expansion reaches us
  // **only through dev tooling** — eslint / @typescript-eslint / glob (minimatch@10),
  // @redocly/openapi-core (minimatch@5), and the hoisted minimatch@3. `npm ls brace-expansion
  // --omit=dev` is empty, and the console bundle rebuilt after the bump is byte-identical
  // (md5 7af44355…), so no version of it has ever shipped to a browser.
  // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // ── Removed 2026-08-08: GHSA-qwww-vcr4-c8h2 (react-router) ─────────────────────────────
  // Same shape as the entry above, and the same lesson. This one read "vulnerable range
  // 7.12.0–8.2.0; there is NO fix in the 7.x line — the fix lands in react-router v8, a
  // different package and a breaking upgrade", and planned a whole migration wave around it.
  // The advisory was re-scoped upstream; its data now says:
  //
  //   react-router >= 7.12.0, < 7.18.2 → 7.18.2   |   >= 8.0.0, < 8.3.0 → 8.3.0
  //
  // The fix was backported into 7.x. `react-router-dom: ^7.9.6` already admits 7.18.2, so a
  // lockfile update closed it — no package.json edit, and no v8 migration. The exception was
  // three weeks from an expiry that would have been met by a breaking upgrade nobody needed.
  //
  // 🔴 This is the second entry in a row removed because its stated reason had gone stale, not
  // because the situation changed in our favour by accident. Upstream backports; a reason
  // written once decays. Re-check by re-running the `gh api` line, never by re-reading prose.
  //
  // Worth keeping from the removed entry (the claim is unchanged and still measured): the admin
  // console is a static SPA built by Vite and served from `public_html/console/`, using
  // `createBrowserRouter` with element-only routes — no RSC mode, no server components, no
  // server-side route `action`/`loader`, no `@react-router/dev` runtime. So the advisory never
  // had a live path here. That is why it was allowlistable at all; it is not why it is gone.
  // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  //
  // The allowlist is EMPTY, and that is the target state. `npm audit` reports 0 vulnerabilities.
  // Every high/critical advisory now fails the build on the day it lands, with no exceptions to
  // reason about. Keep it that way: reach for the fix first (rule 4), and only allowlist when
  // the advisory's own data — not a note about it — says no adoptable fix exists.
  "allowlist": [],
}
